(Regression: refusing `Return a buffer, <text>` (bug #53) must not refuse
 the spellings that always worked. A buffer built inside the function, a
 buffer parameter handed straight back, and a call to another
 buffer-returning function all answer with a real buffer struct, so the
 caller's read of its size is the length that was actually written.)

To 'make a greeting'.
  a buffer called greeting is "ABC".
  Return a buffer, greeting.

To 'hand back' with a buffer called incoming.
  Return a buffer, incoming.

To 'pass along'.
  Return a buffer, 'make a greeting'.

(Two string-initialised buffers: the shape that turned bug #53's
 empty-buffer discrepancy into a segfault, kept so a regression would
 crash here rather than quietly read 0 and pass.)
a buffer called direct is "ABC".
a buffer called second is "DEFGH".

a buffer called made is 'make a greeting'.
print made's size.

a buffer called handed is 'hand back' of second.
print handed's size.

a buffer called relayed is 'pass along'.
print relayed's size.
